course: dictionary
lesson: 10289
title: Torture: a strong word with two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Torture is a strong word. It means causing great pain to someone on purpose, usually to punish them or to make them say something. Read the words around it to see which meaning is being used.
explain: «"Enhanced interrogation" means torture (US).» The brackets tell you where the sentence comes from, and the sentence says that a polite-sounding name can hide a cruel act. Torture is what the name means underneath. Always check: does your meaning fit the sentence?
explain: In everyday talk, people sometimes use torture as a joke. «Waiting for the holidays is torture!» Nobody is being hurt. The speaker means something is very unpleasant or boring. The context tells you which meaning is meant.
explain: Torture can be a noun or a verb. As a noun: «Torture is against international law.» As a verb: «They tortured the prisoner.» The words sheer torture and torture chamber show how the word is used in phrases.
fact: Torture means causing great pain to someone on purpose.
fact: «Torture» can be a noun («torture») or a verb («to torture»; «tortures», «tortured», «torturing»).
fact: In history lessons, torture was used in the past, and it is now against international law.
fact: In joking talk, «this traffic is torture» means something is very unpleasant or boring.
fact: «Sheer torture» means extreme, pure misery, and a «torture chamber» is a place where torture was carried out.
exercise: In history, people were tortured to punish them or to make them talk. What does torture mean? | Something that tastes very sweet | Keeping a secret safe | *Causing great pain on purpose | Giving a prize to the winner
exercise: «Waiting for the holidays is torture!» What does torture mean in this joke? | A happy surprise party | A real and terrible crime | *Something very unpleasant or boring | A holiday in a faraway country
exercise: Which word is a verb form of torture? | sheer | holiday | *tortured | chamber
test: Your friend says a long wait is torture. Are they hurting anyone? What do they mean? | Yes, they mean the wait is on a dangerous path | *No, they mean it is very unpleasant or boring, as a joke | No, they mean a famous old castle | Yes, they mean someone is being hurt on purpose
test: Which phrase means extreme, pure misery? | Quick snack | Gentle breeze | *Sheer torture | Sheer delight
exam: Which sentence about torture is true? | Torture is always a happy word | Torture is a type of castle | *Torture is causing great pain to someone on purpose | Torture only means a joke
